1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,792
This is ECE 320, video 10 a. We fill pipeline diagrams for past exam questions, one cycle at a time.

2
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:30,637
After this video, you can fill a pipeline diagram with and without forwarding. You can explain every stall, every forward and every flush. And you can fill the forwarding table and the stall table. These questions carry the largest part of the midterm marks. Video 10 b continues with more questions.

3
00:00:31,087 --> 00:00:49,860
Here is the method. Go through the instructions in program order. For each source register, first find the producer. The producer is the latest older instruction that writes this register. Skip flushed rows, and skip x0. If there is no producer, there is no hazard.

4
00:00:50,310 --> 00:01:14,054
Next, find two times. When does the value exist? An ALU result exists at the end of X. A load result exists only at the end of M. And when is the value needed? An ALU input or an address is needed in X. Store data is needed in M. A branch with no forwarding into its compare unit needs its operands in D.

5
00:01:14,504 --> 00:01:44,861
Then ask: is there a path in the same cycle? The slides say that the from stage and the to stage are in the same cycle. MX works when the consumer is in X and the producer is in M. The producer must not be a load. WX works when the consumer is in X and the producer is in W. WM is for store data only: the store is in M and the producer is in W. And the register file works when the consumer is in D after the producer's W.

6
00:01:45,311 --> 00:01:51,007
If a path exists, the value is forwarded. You write one row in the forwarding table.

7
00:01:51,457 --> 00:02:03,191
If no path exists, the consumer stalls in D. D repeats, and the repeat gets a star. The instruction behind it repeats F. In the next cycle, test again.

8
00:02:03,641 --> 00:02:19,406
Last, branches. A branch resolves in X. The pipeline guesses not taken. If the branch is taken, the 2 younger instructions are flushed. They get a dash. The target is fetched in the next cycle. This costs 2 cycles.

9
00:02:19,856 --> 00:02:34,192
Let us see the two times in real diagrams. On the left, add writes x1. It computes x1 in X, in cycle 3. So the value exists at the end of cycle 3, in the X M register.

10
00:02:34,642 --> 00:02:48,893
The next instruction, sub, is in X in cycle 4. In cycle 4, add is in M. So x1 goes by MX. The green arrow is vertical, because both stages are in the same cycle.

11
00:02:49,343 --> 00:02:57,108
Two instructions later, and is in X in cycle 5. add is in W. So x1 goes by WX.

12
00:02:57,558 --> 00:03:20,193
Three instructions later, or is in D in cycle 5. In the same cycle, add writes x1 in W. There is no forwarding into D. D sees only values written in earlier cycles. So or reads the old x1, and D repeats once. This is the W D case. In cycle 6, or reads the new value.

13
00:03:20,643 --> 00:03:39,821
On the right, the producer is a load. The load value exists only at the end of M, in cycle 4. sub wants x1 in X in cycle 4. That is too early, even with MX. This is the load-use case. sub stalls one cycle, then gets x1 by WX.

14
00:03:40,271 --> 00:03:59,471
Now look at the instruction and. It is 2 instructions after the load, but the stall moved it. Its D is now in cycle 5, the cycle of the load's W. This is the W D case again, so it stalls once more. Rule: after any stall, compare the real cycles again.

15
00:03:59,921 --> 00:04:21,959
From the same timing facts you get this table. It gives the path, or the number of stalls, for each distance. First row: an ALU result used as an ALU input or an address. One later: MX. Two later: WX. Three later: one stall, the W D case. Four or more later: the register file.

16
00:04:22,409 --> 00:04:36,489
Second row: a load result used in the ALU. One later: one stall, then WX. Two later: WX. Three later: one stall, the W D case. Four later: the register file.

17
00:04:36,939 --> 00:04:56,203
Third row: store data. The store needs the data in M. One later: WM. Two later: two stalls. The reason is that store data has no WX path in the class datapath. So the store must read the register file in D, after the W of the producer.

18
00:04:56,653 --> 00:05:12,695
Last row: no forwarding at all, as in Assignment 3 part a. The same holds for a branch with no compare forwarding. The consumer reads in D after the producer's W. That costs 3, 2, 1 and 0 stalls.

19
00:05:13,145 --> 00:05:23,449
Use this table only when no stall comes between the two instructions. After a stall, compare the real cycles again, as in the last example.

20
00:05:23,899 --> 00:05:37,958
Now the notation. This is slide 47. add has a load-use stall. In cycle 3 it stays in D. That cycle is written D star. A star marks every repeat except the last one.

21
00:05:38,408 --> 00:05:50,931
The instruction behind it, sub, cannot enter D in cycle 3. So it repeats F. That cycle is written F star. Every D stall gives an F stall to the next instruction.

22
00:05:51,381 --> 00:06:15,039
This is part 2, slide 4. The branch B E Q is in X in cycle 4. It resolves there: taken. The pipeline guessed not taken, so it fetched store word and add. These 2 instructions are flushed. They get a dash in their later cycles. The target is fetched in cycle 5, the cycle after X. So a taken branch costs 2 cycles.

23
00:06:15,489 --> 00:06:32,599
Our first question is Assignment 3, question 4. There are 6 instructions. In part a, there is no forwarding. In part b, there is full forwarding, also into the branch compare unit. The branch is not taken. Pause the video and try it.

24
00:06:33,049 --> 00:06:45,379
Step 1 of the method: find the producers. S L T reads t2, which the load writes. B E Q reads t1, which S L T writes. The branch is not taken.

25
00:06:45,829 --> 00:06:58,587
sub reads t2 from the load. add reads t2 from sub, the latest writer. And store word uses t2 as its base address. The producer is add. Nothing writes t3.

26
00:06:59,037 --> 00:07:12,349
Part a, no forwarding. In cycles 1 and 2, the load is fetched and decoded. It reads t3. Nothing older writes t3, so there is no hazard. S L T is fetched in cycle 2.

27
00:07:12,799 --> 00:07:34,921
In cycle 3, S L T is in D. It needs t2. The load writes t2 only in W, in cycle 5. There is no forwarding. So S L T must read t2 in D after cycle 5. S L T repeats D in cycles 3, 4 and 5. B E Q repeats F in the same cycles.

28
00:07:35,371 --> 00:07:48,982
In cycle 6, S L T reads t2 from the register file. The load wrote it in cycle 5, an earlier cycle. So S L T gets the new value. B E Q does its last F.

29
00:07:49,432 --> 00:08:02,851
B E Q needs t1 from S L T. S L T writes t1 in W in cycle 9. So B E Q repeats D in cycles 7, 8 and 9. sub waits in F.

30
00:08:03,301 --> 00:08:20,367
In cycle 10, B E Q reads t1. In cycle 11, B E Q is in X and resolves. It is not taken. The pipeline guessed not taken, so sub is the right next instruction. Nothing is flushed. The add at SKIP is fetched.

31
00:08:20,817 --> 00:08:38,460
sub reads t2 from the load, long after the load's W. So sub has no stall. Then add needs t2 from sub. sub writes t2 in cycle 14. So add repeats D in cycles 12, 13 and 14. store word waits in F.

32
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:50,366
store word uses t2 as its base address. add writes t2 in cycle 18. So store word repeats D in cycles 16, 17 and 18.

33
00:08:50,816 --> 00:09:10,208
In cycle 19, store word reads t2. It finishes W in cycle 22. The run takes 22 cycles. There are 4 RAW hazards, each between neighbours, and each costs 3 stall cycles. This diagram is the same as the published solution.

34
00:09:10,658 --> 00:09:31,607
Part b, with full forwarding. In cycle 3, S L T is in D. Its X would be cycle 4. In cycle 4 the load is in M, and its value exists only at the end of M. So MX cannot help. This is the load-use case. S L T stalls one cycle. B E Q repeats F.

35
00:09:32,057 --> 00:09:42,873
In cycle 5, S L T is in X and the load is in W. t2 goes by WX, from the M W register to the ALU.

36
00:09:43,323 --> 00:09:59,473
In cycle 6, B E Q is in X and S L T is in M. t1 goes by MX. The question says that there is forwarding into the branch compare unit. B E Q resolves not taken, so nothing is flushed.

37
00:09:59,923 --> 00:10:12,125
sub needs t2 from the load. sub is in D in cycle 6. The load wrote t2 in cycle 5. So the register file already has it. No forward is needed.

38
00:10:12,575 --> 00:10:18,271
In cycle 8, add is in X and sub is in M. t2 goes by MX.

39
00:10:18,721 --> 00:10:36,087
In cycle 9, store word is in X and add is in M. store word uses t2 as its address, and the address goes through the ALU. So t2 goes by MX, not by WM. The published solution has the same note.

40
00:10:36,537 --> 00:10:49,379
The run ends in cycle 11. Without forwarding it took 22 cycles. So forwarding gives a speedup of 22 over 11, which is 2. Only the load-use stall is left.

41
00:10:49,829 --> 00:11:14,000
Now a real midterm question: fall 2019, question 2. It has 14 instructions. The pipeline has forwarding, but no forwarding into the decode stage. Branches resolve in X, and the pipeline guesses not taken. The branch in instruction 8 is taken. Part A asks for the diagram and two tables. It is worth 18 marks.

42
00:11:14,450 --> 00:11:32,413
Two facts of the class datapath decide many cells. First, MX and WX feed only the ALU inputs. The store data has only the WM path. Second, there is no forwarding into D. Pause the video and try it. Then continue.

43
00:11:32,863 --> 00:11:42,185
Step 1 of the method: write the producer of each source register. Number 1 and number 2 read registers that nothing older writes.

44
00:11:42,635 --> 00:11:59,553
Number 3 uses x3 as its base address. The producer is the load, number 2. Number 4 add also reads x3 from number 2. Orange means a load producer. That is a warning: a load value exists only at the end of M.

45
00:12:00,003 --> 00:12:18,755
Number 5 and number 6 are stores. Each uses x6 two times: as the base address and as the store data. The producer is add number 4. Number 7 reads x8, and nothing older writes it. Number 8 reads x6 from number 4, and it is taken.

46
00:12:19,205 --> 00:12:43,909
So number 9 and number 10 are on the wrong path. They will be flushed, so they are not producers. That is why number 11 gets x7 from the load number 7, not from number 9. Number 14 reads x7 from number 11, and x8 from the load number 13. Number 13 writes x8 after number 12, so number 13 is the latest producer.

47
00:12:44,359 --> 00:12:59,100
Now fill the diagram. Cycles 1 to 3. Number 1 store word and number 2 load word read x3 and x4. Nothing older writes them, so there is no hazard. Number 3 is fetched in cycle 3.

48
00:12:59,550 --> 00:13:20,265
In cycle 4, number 3 is in D. Its base x3 comes from the load number 2. The X of number 3 would be cycle 5. In cycle 5, the load is in M, and its value exists only at the end of M. This is load-use. Number 3 stalls one cycle. Number 4 repeats F.

49
00:13:20,715 --> 00:13:50,261
In cycle 6, number 3 is in X and the load is in W. x3 goes by WX. In the same cycle, number 4 add is in D, and it also needs x3. The load writes x3 in this cycle, but D sees only earlier writes. So number 4 reads the old x3. This is the W D case. Number 4 repeats D. In cycle 7 the value is no longer in M W, so no path can reach X. Number 5 repeats F.

50
00:13:50,711 --> 00:13:59,970
In cycle 7, number 4 reads x3 again from the register file. Now it gets the new value. Number 5 does its last F.

51
00:14:00,420 --> 00:14:17,444
Number 5 store word uses x6 as base and as data. In cycle 9, number 5 is in X and add number 4 is in M. The base x6 goes by MX. Number 6 is in D, and it waits. The next step shows why.

52
00:14:17,894 --> 00:14:59,211
In cycle 10, number 5 is in M and number 4 is in W. The store data x6 goes by WM. Now number 6. It is also a store with x6 as base and data. In cycle 9, its X would be cycle 10, while number 4 is in W. The base could use WX. But store data has no WX path, and WM would need number 6 in M in cycle 10. So number 6 cannot go on. In cycle 10, number 6 is in D in the cycle of number 4's W: the W D case. So it repeats D again. Number 7 waits in F.

53
00:14:59,661 --> 00:15:09,495
In cycle 11, number 6 reads x6 from the register file. So number 6 has 2 stall cycles. Number 7 does its last F.

54
00:15:09,945 --> 00:15:26,735
Cycles 12 and 13. Number 7 load word reads x8, and nothing older writes x8. Number 8 B E Q reads x6 and x8. Number 4 wrote x6 in cycle 10, long before. So there is no hazard.

55
00:15:27,185 --> 00:15:39,153
In cycle 14, B E Q is in X. It resolves: taken. The pipeline guessed not taken, so it fetched number 9 and number 10. Both are on the wrong path.

56
00:15:39,603 --> 00:15:55,496
In cycle 15, number 9 and number 10 are flushed. They get a dash in each later cycle. The target T1, instruction 11, is fetched in cycle 15, the cycle after X. The taken branch costs 2 cycles.

57
00:15:55,946 --> 00:16:12,735
Number 11 needs x7. Number 9 also writes x7, but number 9 is flushed. So the producer is the load number 7. It wrote x7 in cycle 15. Number 11 reads it in D in cycle 16. No stall.

58
00:16:13,185 --> 00:16:24,855
In cycles 17 and 18, number 12 and number 13 read only x0. x0 is never a hazard. Number 14 is fetched in cycle 18.

59
00:16:25,305 --> 00:16:51,822
Number 14 add needs x7 and x8. x8 comes from the load number 13. The X of number 14 would be cycle 20, when the load is in M: load-use. x7 comes from number 11. Number 11 is in W in cycle 19, the same cycle as this D: the W D case. Both reasons ask for a stall in cycle 19. One D star covers both.

60
00:16:52,272 --> 00:17:06,373
In cycle 20, number 14 reads x7 from the register file. In cycle 21, number 14 is in X and the load number 13 is in W. x8 goes by WX.

61
00:17:06,823 --> 00:17:16,231
Number 14 finishes in cycle 23. The run takes 23 cycles. There are 4 forwards and 5 stall cycles in D.

62
00:17:16,681 --> 00:17:32,063
Now the forwarding table. Each forward is one row. Row 1: source number 2, destination number 3, register x3, from W to X, in cycle 6. The picture below shows the two rows in that cycle.

63
00:17:32,513 --> 00:17:41,579
Row 2: from number 4 to number 5, x6, from M to X, in cycle 9. This is the base address of number 5.

64
00:17:42,029 --> 00:17:55,939
Row 3: from number 4 to number 5 again, x6, from W to M, in cycle 10. This is the store data of number 5. One instruction can receive two forwards, one for each source.

65
00:17:56,389 --> 00:18:10,362
Row 4: from number 13 to number 14, x8, from W to X, in cycle 21. Note that there is no forward for x7 into number 14. It came from the register file.

66
00:18:10,812 --> 00:18:27,516
Now the stall table. Each stall gets one row with its stage, its cycles and the reason. Number 3 stalls in D in cycle 4: load-use on x3. Because number 3 holds D, number 4 stalls in F in cycle 4.

67
00:18:27,966 --> 00:18:44,222
Number 4 stalls in D in cycle 6. The reason is the W D case. Number 2 writes x3 in W in cycle 6. D in the same cycle reads the old value. Number 5 stalls in F in cycle 6.

68
00:18:44,672 --> 00:19:03,424
Number 6 stalls in D in cycles 9 and 10. Its store data x6 has only the WM path, and WM is not possible in time. So number 6 reads x6 in D after cycle 10. Number 7 stalls in F in cycles 9 and 10.

69
00:19:03,874 --> 00:19:25,165
Number 14 stalls in D in cycle 19. There are two reasons: load-use on x8 from number 13, and the W D case on x7 from number 11. Write both reasons. The flushed instructions number 9 and number 10 do not go in the stall table. They go in the diagram, with dashes.

70
00:19:25,615 --> 00:19:35,407
Here is the recap of video 10 a. One. For each source register, find the producer, the time the value exists, and the time it is needed.

71
00:19:35,857 --> 00:19:49,766
Two. A path works only when both stages are in the same cycle. MX, but not from a load. WX. WM for store data. Or the register file, after the producer's W.

72
00:19:50,216 --> 00:19:59,560
Three. If there is no path, the consumer stalls in D, and the instruction behind it stalls in F. Test again in the next cycle.

73
00:20:00,010 --> 00:20:08,437
Four. There is no forwarding into D. A D in the cycle of the producer's W reads the old value and stalls once.

74
00:20:08,887 --> 00:20:17,249
Five. In the class datapath, store data has only the WM path. Two instructions later, it costs 2 stalls.

75
00:20:17,699 --> 00:20:25,230
Six. A taken branch resolves in X. Two rows get dashes, and the target is fetched in the next cycle.

76
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:39,525
Seven. After any stall, compare the real cycles again. Flushed rows are never producers. Video 10 b continues with Part B of this question and with the fall 2022 paper.
